speechtext:
I want to say a few words on this bill, and I would ask hon. members to look at it in this way. The government did a good stroke of business when they enacted the gold mining assistance act. I shall cite only three examples-Dome Mines Limited, Hollinger Consolidated Gold Mines Limited, and McIntyre Porcupine Mines Limited. In the case of Dome Mines Limited the government paid assistance to the amount of $1.27 per ounce. In the case of Hollinger they paid assistance to the amount of $2.37 an ounce. In the case of McIntyre Porcupine Mines they paid assistance amounting to $1.93 an ounce. The gold which was produced by these different mines was worth $35 an ounce. Therefore for each ounce of gold the government paid these mines respectively $36.27, $37.37 and $36.93. I think the government made a very good deal all through, and by helping the other mines which were producing gold at very high cost they helped also the people in the towns which were developed by these mines.
I feel sorry that the legislation which we are studying today is not going further. It should be extended for three years, to encourage the development of new gold mines;

because we know that by encouraging prospecting for gold mines we also encourage prospecting for base metals, and base metals are certainly very important.
